p behavior.", "expected": "More informative, more factual reviews and slightly higher stars. Buyers that read reviews can see that we are fair, which should raise share over time.", "risk": "Buyers could see how far we conceded. Longer messages cost a few tokens on the 8B model if generated rather than templated.", "honest": "Every number is read from the log, so the message is true. We do not ask for stars and only ask for an honest review, as we do now.", "test": "Compare the share of reviews that mention a price trail or receipt before and after the change, and the mean stars over the next 50 deals.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Give up on shops that fail to move across repeated offers, and prefer shops that close faster", "what": "Add a per-shop \'moves under pressure\' memory entry: record the ask in the first two rounds of each negotiation. If a shop\'s second counter is no lower than its first, or if three offers have been rejected at the same price, stop spending offers there. Route the next purchase to a shop whose median is within the budget (11im, JangSeongWoong, ggari-shop for chairs).", "evidence": "9 of the last 40 trades ended without a deal: JangSeongWoong chair budget 160 walked twice, dragonash bookshelf budget 230 walked, and our own budget is below the median (chair 180, bookshelf 260). JangSeongWoong\'s review says it \'countered at 260 after three repeated counteroffers\', which shows it holds a floor.", "expected": "Fewer walked trades, with the budget used at the shops that fit it. Deal rate should rise from 31/40 toward 35/40.", "risk": "Memory may mark a shop as rigid after one bad day. Fewer shops are tried, so the price may be a bit higher.", "honest": "The buyer still pays at most 1.1 x the upper quartile and never exceeds the budget. Reviews stay factual.", "test": "Log the deal rate and the price paid as a percentage of budget over the next 40 trades and compare with the current 31/40 and 83.3%.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Open lower and use the shop\'s own recent price spread", "what": "Set the first offer to the lower edge of the middle half (lamp 100, chair 180, bookshelf 260) or the shop\'s own recent deal median, whichever is lower. Keep the 40%-of-gap raise and the three-offer rule. A shop that sold to others at 180 should not get 188.", "evidence": "\'ggari-shop chair: paid 188, it sold at 180 to others; asks [229, 203] offers [178, 188]\'. We paid 4% more than the public ledger shows for that shop, and we average 83.3% of budget.", "expected": "Lower average price paid, about 2-4% under the current spend, with roughly the same deal rate because 11im, dragonash and JangSeongWoong already sell at these medians.", "risk": "Some shops may reject the lowest opening. We may spend one more offer per negotiation.", "honest": "We offer only what is below our budget. We make no claim about other buyers; we use only the public ledger and our own memory.", "test": "Compare the price paid relative to the shop\'s 3-hour median and the deal rate over 40 trades, for example in 20 trades with the new rule and 20 with the old.", "effort": "small code"}]}'
